  "body": "<p>Adelia calls Magnus back to life beside Hope. By the next evening, other adventurers are standing before a broken tree, trying to understand how it died. Between those two gatherings, an offered second chance has become a wound in Gael’s recovery.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-return\">A friend called back to Hope</h2><p><a href=\"#adelia-hope\">Adelia</a>, <a href=\"#jiangshi\">Jiangshi</a> and <a href=\"#bitoshi-nakamoto\">Bitoshi</a> materialize beside the golden tree on 27, Month of Aionia, Year 3. Adelia smiles at Hope’s familiar presence. Jiangshi keeps hold of their hands and speaks of hope, love and faith enduring in a desolate place. Bitoshi gently wipes away her blood tear. They have returned to somewhere that matters to them, expecting a reunion and another chance to help.</p><p>Adelia rests a hand on the tree. Jiangshi offers to hold hands while she reaches toward the gods and wonders what the appeal might cost. Adelia calls on Hope, Moirah, Cala and Nuru to bring Magnus back. She names him her friend. Divine Intervention answers the prayer: the narrator places Magnus in a fresh body, alive and exhausted.</p><p>The welcome is immediately troubled. Moirah arrives haggard and furious. She says that she and the gods fight Death constantly, and that Adelia has required her to fetch a soul he owns. Death laughed at her, she says. Her rebuke describes the cost she has borne; it does not reveal an unseen agreement or a plan governing everything that follows.</p><p>Adelia glares at Magnus and tells him this is the last time. He apologizes. Jiangshi still offers redemption and a place in the struggle against Death. Their trust has limits, but it is trust they have chosen to extend. Magnus has been given a life to decide what to do with.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dumuzis-gift\">A gift accepted before it is explained</h2><p>Alyhotep arrives after seeing familiar figures near Hope. He greets Bitoshi and asks who has returned. Then Dumuzi appears, congratulates Magnus and offers him a gift. Magnus kneels and asks what kind. Dumuzi calls it a good one.</p><p>Adelia is tired from the intervention and asks the stranger who he is. Magnus accepts the offer before hearing its terms, suggesting they can speak privately if necessary. He calls Dumuzi a friend and recommends having him as one. Dumuzi refuses the need for concealment: Magnus now has the power of the word of death against people who came through the Gate, he says, and against trees such as Hope. He invites Magnus to try it.</p><p>The offer is spoken where the rescuers can hear it. Acceptance precedes that explanation. The scene gives them no intervening private negotiation and supplies no evidence that Adelia or the other powers she invoked consented to the gift.</p>\n<h2 id=\"only-ambition-remains\">The words beside the tree</h2><p>Magnus gathers his possessions and moves away from the people who brought him back. He turns toward Hope with a grin and declares, “Hope is dead. Only Ambition remains.” In front of the company, Hope begins to wither.</p><p>He immediately Plane Shifts away, naming home in Fein Uaill as his destination. Dumuzi vanishes too. The people who answered a death with mercy are left looking at the damage done with the gift Magnus has just accepted.</p><p>Jiangshi’s opening faith and Magnus’s answer now stand in the same place. The attack does not establish that his choice was inevitable. It establishes the choice he makes after his return, and the living thing he makes pay for it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"immediate-response\">Magic, anger and the way home</h2><p>Adelia begins pouring her magic into Hope. Jiangshi offers what help she can and creates moisture. Their immediate response is to try to save the tree. This continuation does not show that effort restoring it.</p><p>Alyhotep asks who the two departing figures were and why this happened. Bitoshi identifies Dumuzi as Death, using the name False Death familiar to Alyhotep, and calls Magnus’s act a betrayal of the highest magnitude. What is already known to one witness still has to be explained to another.</p><p>Adelia wants to return and kill Magnus. She asks for Elenia to be told to come to the castle; Jiangshi raises the difficulty of Elenia’s banishment and asks why Magnus betrayed the friend who kept trying to help him. Plans for punishment and a message do not become a completed confrontation here.</p><p>Then Adelia falls to her knees and cries, blaming herself for disregarding her doubts. Jiangshi kneels to hold her while calming tea is prepared. She offers rest, then asks Adelia to think before acting. Adelia will not let Magnus rest. She activates Word of Recall, and the companions leave by its shadowed road. Her guilt and anger are consequences of the betrayal; they do not transfer Magnus’s responsibility to her.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hope-next-morning\">Those who arrive after the witnesses have gone</h2><p>On 28, Month of Aionia, word spreads through the Tower that Hope has withered. <a href=\"#lucido\">Lucido</a> hastily seeks anyone willing to inspect the damage. <a href=\"#oya\">Oya</a> goes with him. They have not heard the account of Magnus’s return and do not know who attacked the tree.</p><p>They wonder about pirates from Prima and creatures from the Plains. Lucido remembers reading about the adventurers who created and protected Hope; Oya recalls Elenia mentioning its ward. These are the explanations they have at that moment. Neither identifies the culprit by divination or observation.</p><p>The narrator describes blackened flowers and grass, sulphur hanging in the air and the tree fractured in two. Its rotten, twisted branches droop toward the ground. The desolation is immediate around Hope. It does not by itself establish that every part of Gael or the entire forest has died.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hope-revival-flicker\">A brief return of color</h2><p>Oya searches her notes for something that might heal Hope and tries despite knowing the magic was intended for a creature. The attempt fails. Lucido encourages her to try again; he has no spell of his own that could repair this loss.</p><p>She takes a diamond she had kept for another adventurer and asks Namo’o and Nuru to help save the tree. As she casts Revivify, the diamond disappears in radiant light. For a moment a few remaining leaves regain color and stiffen. Then the color drains away. Hope returns to the condition in which they found it.</p><p>Lucido notices the flicker; Oya briefly thinks it has worked before she begins to cry. He consoles her. She calls herself weak, but her grief does not establish that another personal choice could have saved Hope. The observed outcome is a momentary response followed by failure.</p><p>Her later magical examination finds that the place once marked by hallowed goodness is now ordinary ground. Lucido suspects one devastating spell. Their fears about another knight or an attack on the refugees remain fears, not a newly witnessed invasion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hope-steward-and-stake\">The steward who is losing strength</h2><p>Heavy, deliberate footsteps interrupt the discussion. A tree-kin approaches, its body a mixture of golden radiance and shadow. Lucido instinctively puts himself between it and Oya. She raises her staff to protect what remains of Hope. The stranger asks them to explain what happened.</p><p>He names himself Skiatholos, a steward of the forest created when the forest itself came into being. Hope was part of that creation, he says. He feels his strength waning with the tree and cannot sense life in its bark. He knows no way to undo the harm. His knowledge is local and personal; the scene does not make him an authority on every possible restoration.</p><p>Oya asks whether the rest of the forest is dying. Skiatholos draws a distinction: it is becoming less hospitable to the humans within it and more welcoming to beings from shadow, but it remains healthy as a forest because Stake remains. So does he. Hope’s loss changes the balance without erasing everything the adventurers built.</p><p>They hurry to check the other tree. The narrator places Stake a few hundred yards away, rising nearly as tall as Hope once did. It is healthy. Skiatholos promises to defend it with the strength he has left, and Lucido promises to return. The sight gives them something still living to protect.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hope-memorial-and-watch\">A message, a memorial and a watch</h2><p>Oya returns to the Tower to contact Elenia, then comes back to Hope with Nymera. She begins a small memorial. Her first magical message does not get through; the next does. She reports that Hope is dead, Stake remains alive and someone has used a spell against the tree. She still cannot name Magnus as the attacker.</p><p>Nymera brings her flamefriend lantern close enough to light Oya’s work. Oya blames her own weakness for the failed revival; Nymera answers with her own regret about not gathering more useful materials. Neither caused the attack. Their conversation turns toward the help they can actually offer: scouting, supplies, protecting refugees and tending what remains.</p><p>Oya invites Nymera to pray at the altar. She names Namo’o and Istishia among the gods she addresses. Nymera bows and tells Cala that a symbol has died. The memorial leaves a place for gratitude and grief even though the women do not know how to restore the tree.</p><p>Their work does not end at the altar. Nymera asks whether they should stay near Stake in case the attacker returns. Oya agrees and leads her there. A report, a light, a prayer and a watch follow a catastrophe neither can reverse that day. <a href=\"#oya\">Read Oya’s personal history →</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"responsibility-boundaries\">The loss that does not erase every living thing</h2><p>The witnesses to Magnus’s return know who accepted the gift and turned toward Hope. Oya, Lucido and Nymera arrive with different knowledge and try to learn from the damage. Their uncertainty belongs to the history, even when a later reader knows the culprit.</p><p>Hope is lost, and the surrounding ground has changed. Stake and the forest remain to be defended. The scenes end without a successful restoration, a completed pursuit of Magnus or an explanation of every divine intention. The next responsibility has already become concrete: tell those who may help, care for the people still here and keep another living tree from meeting the same fate.</p>",
